    <!-- 2. What Changes on November 1 -->
    <section id="what-changes" class="content-section">
      <h2>What Changes on November 1</h2>
      <p>
        Every autumn, tens of millions of households across North America take part in the annual ritual of setting their clocks back by one hour. In the United States and Canada, the transition marks the formal conclusion of Daylight Saving Time (DST) and the return of standard astronomical time. On Sunday, November 1, 2026, Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4) officially ends, and Eastern Standard Time (EST, UTC-5) resumes, governing wall clocks until the second Sunday of March 2027.
      </p>
      <p>
        Under federal legislation outlined in the Energy Policy Act of 2005, the changeover takes place at precisely 2:00 AM local time. As digital and analog timekeepers reach 1:59:59 AM, the subsequent second does not tick forward to 2:00:00 AM; instead, it rolls back sixty minutes to 1:00:00 AM. Because of this artificial one-hour retreat, the calendar day of Sunday, November 1 contains 25 hours instead of the customary 24. For workers, parents, and students, the immediate personal reward is an extra sixty minutes of sleep before beginning the new week.
      </p>
      <p>
        Astronomically and meteorologically, the shift alters the relationship between solar noon and civil clocks. By moving clocks backward, sunrise occurs approximately one hour earlier according to wall clocks, brightening early commutes and school transit. The trade-off is an accelerated dusk: evening twilight vanishes noticeably sooner, plunging late afternoons into twilight darkness. For commuters on the East Coast, daylight that previously extended until nearly 6:00 PM EDT abruptly disappears shortly after 4:45 PM EST.
      </p>
      <p>
        Crucially for engineers, global finance desks, logistics dispatchers, and international remote workers, Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) itself never changes. GMT represents a constant astronomical standard anchored to zero degrees longitude at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London (sharing the identical numerical offset as UTC+0). Because GMT never adjusts for seasonal shifts, the mathematical gap between GMT and North America's Eastern Time zone expands from 4 hours during summer back to 5 hours during standard winter time.
      </p>
      <p>
        To understand the complete mathematical relationship between seasonal offsets, explore our detailed reference on <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT</a>, or convert any live time immediately across global regions using our interactive <a href="/">homepage converter</a>.
      </p>

