      <h2>Key Conversions: Before vs. After November 1</h2>
      <p>
        The one-hour backward adjustment transforms every recurring transatlantic meeting schedule, global broadcast window, and financial market handoff. Because the UK, European continent, and international timestamp servers operate on strict Greenwich coordinates, everyday business formulas must be updated.
      </p>
      <p>
        During the summer months, Eastern Daylight Time sits 4 hours behind Greenwich Mean Time. When clocks fall back on November 1, Eastern Standard Time drops to 5 hours behind GMT. A scheduled recurring daily synchronization that occurred at 12:00 PM GMT matched 8:00 AM EDT in October, but suddenly lands at 7:00 AM EST in November. For team members in New York, Boston, or Toronto, this means logging on one full clock hour earlier in the morning to catch their European counterparts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The table below provides a comprehensive six-point benchmark comparison across the standard global business day, showing both the summer (EDT) and winter (EST) conversions against fixed GMT reference points:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
