      <p>
        Notice in particular how the afternoon window compresses. When European financial exchanges close at 5:00 PM GMT, New York traders are at 1:00 PM EDT prior to November 1, leaving three solid hours of active Wall Street trading before the 4:00 PM close. After the clocks fall back, 5:00 PM GMT coincides with 12:00 PM EST, providing four hours of post-European trading on the US East Coast.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. The Gap Week: October 25 - November 1 -->
    <section id="gap-week" class="content-section">
      <h2>The Gap Week: October 25 – November 1</h2>
      <p>
        Perhaps the most confusing period on the international calendar occurs during the seven days separating the European and North American clock transitions. The United Kingdom and the European Union operate under European Union Directive 2000/84/EC, which mandates that summer time begins on the final Sunday of March and ends on the final Sunday of October. In 2026, the European fall-back date occurs on Sunday, October 25.
      </p>
      <p>
        In contrast, United States and Canadian federal guidelines established by the Energy Policy Act of 2005 delay the autumn clock transition until the first Sunday in November. In 2026, the North American fall back happens on Sunday, November 1.
      </p>
      <p>
        This regulatory divergence produces an annual seven-day "gap week." During this week from October 25 to November 1, Europe has already fallen back to standard astronomical time (switching from BST to GMT in the UK, and CEST to CET across Western Europe), while North America remains stubbornly on daylight saving time (EDT).
      </p>
      <p>
        As a consequence, the normal geographical time differences contract by exactly one hour:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>London to New York</strong>: Usually separated by 5 hours during the winter, the difference shrinks to just 4 hours during the gap week.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paris, Berlin &amp; Rome to New York</strong>: Normally 6 hours ahead of Eastern Time during the winter, these European capitals sit only 5 hours ahead of New York.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Dublin to Toronto</strong>: Contracts from 5 hours down to 4 hours.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This temporary contraction wreaks havoc on international corporate workflows. A weekly cross-border video conference configured in calendar software according to London local time will take place one hour earlier for New York attendees during this week. Conversely, an event tied to New York local time will force European attendees to dial in one hour later than usual. International airline flight manifests, transoceanic cargo tracking, and broadcast television schedules must all apply temporary translation patches during this specific 7-day window.
      </p>

